// Title: Capsh Shell Invocation - Linux
// Sigma ID: db1ac3be-f606-4e3a-89e0-9607cbe6b98a
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Li Ling, Andy Parkidomo, Robert Rakowski, Blake Hartstein (Bloomberg L.P.)
// Date: 2024-09-02 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, T1059
// Description: Detects the use of the "capsh" utility to invoke a shell.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/capsh/#shell
//   - https://github.com/elastic/detection-rules/blob/0aacc5e19292f764a002ea2093b2a076db35b589/rules/_deprecated/execution_shell_evasion_linux_binary.toml
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/d7c9da10c170a6988d68ebdf2c9f7bd8782b7c55/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_capsh_shell_invocation.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/capsh" and ProcessCommandLine endswith " --"
